{"task_id": "Mbpp/125", "solution": "def find_length(binary_string: str) -> int:\n    # Transforms the binary string to an array where '0' becomes 1 and '1' becomes -1.\n    # Then applies Kadane's algorithm to find the maximum subarray sum.\n    \n    if not isinstance(binary_string, str) or not all(c in '01' for c in binary_string):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be a binary string consisting only of '0's and '1's.\")\n\n    if not binary_string:\n        return 0\n\n    transformed_arr = [1 if char == '0' else -1 for char in binary_string]\n\n    max_so_far = float('-inf')\n    current_max = 0\n    \n    # If all elements are negative (all 1s in original string), the max difference is the largest individual element (i.e., -1).\n    # If there's at least one '0', current_max will eventually be positive or zero.\n    \n    has_zero = False\n    for x in transformed_arr:\n        if x == 1:\n            has_zero = True\n        \n        current_max += x\n        if current_max > max_so_far:\n            max_so_far = current_max\n        if current_max < 0: # Reset if current sum becomes negative\n            current_max = 0\n            \n    # Handle the case where all elements are -1 (all '1's in original string).\n    # In this specific case, Kadane's standard algo would return 0, but we need -1 (for single '1').\n    # The requirement is max difference (0s - 1s). For \"1\", it's -1. For \"11\", it's -1.\n    # The logic above correctly sets current_max to 0 if it goes below 0. \n    # If the input contains only '1's, transformed_arr contains only -1s.\n    # max_so_far will be initialized to -inf and will correctly track the largest single -1.\n    # However, if max_so_far is still -inf after the loop, it means the string was empty.\n    # No, if string contains only '1's, max_so_far will be updated to -1 (the first element), \n    # then current_max will become 0 again. If it never finds a positive sum, then the result should be max of individual elements.\n    \n    # Let's adjust for the edge case: what if all resulting values are negative?\n    # e.g., \"111\" -> [-1, -1, -1]. Max sub-array sum here is -1, not 0.\n    # Kadane's algorithm typically returns 0 for a sequence of all negative numbers if an empty subarray is allowed.\n    # Here, we want at least a single element subarray.\n    \n    if not has_zero: # If the original string had no '0's, then the max difference will always be negative (or 0 for empty string handled above).\n        return -1 # Max difference for any substring of \"111\" is -1 (e.g. \"1\" -> 0s:0, 1s:1, diff:-1).\n\n    # If the string contains '0's and current_max was always reset to 0 whenever it dropped below 0,\n    # the max_so_far will represent the max positive difference.\n    # If current_max never became positive, it means no substring had more 0s than 1s.\n    # For example, \"101\" -> [-1, 1, -1]. current_max sequence: -1->0 (reset), 1->1, 0 (reset). max_so_far=1.\n    # Example \"11100\" -> [-1,-1,-1,1,1]. current_max: -1 -> 0, -1 -> 0, -1 -> 0, 1->1, 2->2. max_so_far=2.\n    \n    # If string is \"11\", transformed is [-1, -1]. current_max: -1 (reset to 0), -1 (reset to 0). max_so_far remains -inf.\n    # This implies the logic for all negative numbers needs to be more robust.\n    # Let's use the standard Kadane's that finds max of a non-empty subarray.\n    \n    max_so_far_kadane = transformed_arr[0]\n    current_max_kadane = transformed_arr[0]\n    \n    for i in range(1, len(transformed_arr)):\n        current_max_kadane = max(transformed_arr[i], current_max_kadane + transformed_arr[i])\n        max_so_far_kadane = max(max_so_far_kadane, current_max_kadane)\n        \n    return max_so_far_kadane"}
