{"task_id": "Mbpp/615", "solution": "def average_tuple(data: tuple[tuple[int, ...], ...]) -> list[float]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the average value for each inner tuple within a tuple of tuples.\n    Returns a list of these averages as floats.\n    \"\"\"\n    averages = []\n    for inner_tuple in data:\n        # Calculate the sum and count of elements in the inner tuple, then compute their average.\n        # Division in Python 3 automatically results in a float.\n        average = sum(inner_tuple) / len(inner_tuple)\n        averages.append(average)\n    return averages"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/616", "solution": "def tuple_modulo(tuple1: tuple, tuple2: tuple) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"\n    Performs an element-wise modulo operation on two input tuples.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(tuple1, tuple) or not isinstance(tuple2, tuple):\n        raise TypeError(\"Both arguments must be tuples.\")\n\n    if len(tuple1) != len(tuple2):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input tuples must have the same length.\")\n\n    result_elements = []\n    for i in range(len(tuple1)):\n        element1 = tuple1[i]\n        element2 = tuple2[i]\n\n        if not isinstance(element1, (int, float)) or not isinstance(element2, (int, float)):\n            raise TypeError(f\"Tuple elements must be numerical. Found non-numerical at index {i}.\")\n\n        if element2 == 0:\n            raise ZeroDivisionError(f\"Modulo by zero is not allowed at index {i}.\")\n\n        result_elements.append(element1 % element2)\n\n    return tuple(result_elements)"}
